WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.236
- [Helga] Arnold!

00:00:01.236 --> 00:00:02.600
- [Narrator] Remember your favorite

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.499
football shaped cartoon from the ’90s?

00:00:04.499 --> 00:00:06.637
Well, this is Craig Bartlett, the

00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:09.774
non-football shaped creator of Hey Arnold!

00:00:09.774 --> 00:00:12.403
So we thought we'd ask him
a few things about the show.

00:00:12.403 --> 00:00:15.320
But before we start, Craig,
can you do what you do best?

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.464
- So do you want me to, uh,
just start drawing Arnold?

00:00:17.464 --> 00:00:18.654
- [Narrator] We sure do.

00:00:18.654 --> 00:00:19.866
- Alright, here we go.

00:00:19.866 --> 00:00:22.866
(suspenseful music)

00:00:28.738 --> 00:00:30.796
When I designed Arnold in the first place,

00:00:30.796 --> 00:00:34.036
I was really trying to make him stand out.

00:00:34.036 --> 00:00:36.189
I always remember what Matt Groening told me,

00:00:36.189 --> 00:00:39.448
he said, “You should
try to make them so that

00:00:39.448 --> 00:00:42.634
they work in silhouette or
really small on the screen."

00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:44.176
So that's how it started;
Arnold was a little clay

00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:47.676
football shaped head because it was funny.

00:00:48.576 --> 00:00:50.453
- Move it, football head!

00:00:50.453 --> 00:00:51.827
- Hey Arnold!

00:00:51.827 --> 00:00:55.400
- It was just me trying
to be kinda radical.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.757
Pigeon man, that is a pet peeve of mine.

00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:00.698
- [Narrator] In one
episode, Arnold meets the

00:01:00.698 --> 00:01:02.938
lonely pigeon man and at
the end of the episode...

00:01:02.938 --> 00:01:04.474
- Where will you go?

00:01:04.474 --> 00:01:06.954
- Somewhere I can live
in peace with my friends.

00:01:06.954 --> 00:01:09.333
- [Narrator] Well, the internet
had some ideas about that.

00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:10.992
- Somebody said it's a suicide story,

00:01:10.992 --> 00:01:15.072
that he jumps off the building
and Arnold in his mind

00:01:15.072 --> 00:01:18.850
to make sense out of it,
imagines that he flew away.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.856
I hate that, we wrote what we wrote

00:01:21.856 --> 00:01:23.813
because I wanted him to fly away,

00:01:23.813 --> 00:01:27.232
I always imagine pigeon
man going to Paris next,

00:01:27.232 --> 00:01:30.192
where he helps pigeons in Paris.

00:01:30.192 --> 00:01:31.930
I just really wanna set
the record straight,

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.680
pigeon man is not a
suicide story, he's fine.

00:01:37.059 --> 00:01:39.317
I wanted to make Hey Arnold! have

00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:43.034
a really detailed specific vibe of a city

00:01:43.034 --> 00:01:45.194
that was kinda ugly but beautiful.

00:01:45.194 --> 00:01:48.874
Arnold's city is based on
where I grew up in Seattle

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:51.637
and also, where I went
to art school in Portland

00:01:51.637 --> 00:01:54.336
but it's also based on
Brooklyn with all the stoops

00:01:54.336 --> 00:01:57.456
and the brownstones, so
it's really a kind of a

00:01:57.456 --> 00:02:00.373
collage of several northern cities.

00:02:02.641 --> 00:02:05.878
When people talk about what
they like best about the show,

00:02:05.878 --> 00:02:08.577
the room comes up over and over again,

00:02:08.577 --> 00:02:12.081
which is a super cool
attic with the skylight

00:02:12.081 --> 00:02:15.339
and then he can climb right
up the stairs on the wall

00:02:15.339 --> 00:02:17.878
and open the window of his skylight

00:02:17.878 --> 00:02:20.729
and go down the fire
escape and we were reading,

00:02:20.729 --> 00:02:23.867
Gabriel García Márquez
and One Hundred Years of Solitude

00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:25.550
and Love in the Time of Cholera.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.355
Those were big influences on us because

00:02:27.355 --> 00:02:30.206
magic realism is the term coined about

00:02:30.206 --> 00:02:31.822
García Márquez’s writing.

00:02:31.822 --> 00:02:34.323
I wanted there to be a magical realism

00:02:34.323 --> 00:02:37.573
to Arnold's house and his neighborhood.

00:02:41.885 --> 00:02:43.885
- [Narrator] So Craig, one last question.

00:02:43.885 --> 00:02:44.899
- You bet.

00:02:44.899 --> 00:02:46.210
- [Narrator] After all those years

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.002
of making TV for kids, was it worth it?

00:02:48.002 --> 00:02:50.184
- Making TV is incredibly powerful, you know.

00:02:50.184 --> 00:02:53.624
Having millions of people
watch what you make on TV,

00:02:53.624 --> 00:02:56.642
you realize what you
say, especially to a kid,

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:58.320
they're gonna soak that up and

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.800
they're never gonna forget it.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.272
One of the themes in the series is that,

00:03:01.272 --> 00:03:03.944
you know, you're kind of
trying to make the most

00:03:03.944 --> 00:03:05.762
out of a not so good situation.

00:03:05.762 --> 00:03:07.624
That's really what the show's about.

00:03:07.624 --> 00:03:10.082
I'll get this really
heartfelt notes from people

00:03:10.082 --> 00:03:12.541
and they'll say that they got strength

00:03:12.541 --> 00:03:15.323
when things looked their worst.

00:03:15.323 --> 00:03:16.523
What can be better than that?

00:03:16.523 --> 00:03:19.083
That's incredibly gratifying.

00:03:19.083 --> 00:03:20.833
- [Helga] Hey Arnold!

